A palette a program sets wins over every target's own answer.

Unset, html and gtk4 draw code in the default palette and the other three ask
their host's theme. Set through `markup.palette`, every target draws the
palette it was given, which is what lets an application with colors of its own
see them in its code samples.

A constant palette is a literal by the time each emitter sees it, because an
inlined body reads a provider's value in place of the context: gtk4 and fyne
build the color into the span, html writes it onto the element, bubbletea and
android name it in the style. A palette read from state is the run-time half,
and is markup_palette_reactive.txtar.

The override reading the context is what reached codegen as a bare
`ContextRead` before: `passContext` rewrites reads only in the components it
lists, and a bodiless library component was listed only when its override
declared state.

deny out/go/gtk4/main.go `<tt>func main</tt>` -- the words are colored; a color the build could not read used to be dropped
deny out/none/html/index.html `setAttribute\("(spanStyle|classStyle|classStyleDark)"` -- a run's style is CSS, never an attribute
deny out/go/bubbletea/model.go `Foreground\(lipgloss\.Color\("[0-9]+"\)\)` -- the palette's color, not the terminal's
deny * `__ctx_palette` -- the provider's value is read in place, not copied into a hidden field

-- main.sngl --
import ui "sngl:ui"
import markup "sngl:ui/markup"

output {
    go {
        gtk4
        bubbletea
        fyne
    }
    kotlin {
        android
    }
    none {
        html
    }
}

ui.window #home(title="t") {
    markup.palette(markup.darkPalette) {
        markup.richText {
            markup.monospace {
                markup.token(kind=markup.Token.keyword) {
                    markup.text("func")
                }
                markup.text(" ")
                markup.token(kind=markup.Token.function) {
                    markup.text("main")
                }
            }
        }
    }
}
